### S3 — APS version of record, pp. 107502-2–3

Here tildes denote the correction itself, not effective totals. Units: c=ℏ=k_B=1; κ=8πG=m_P⁻², with m_P reduced Planck mass. a(t) is a closed FLRW scale factor, k=1, dot=d/dt, ε and p ordinary density/pressure, n fermion number density, **s** spatial Dirac spin pseudovector.

The unnumbered closure before (10) is
\[
\langle{\bf s}^2\rangle=\frac34n^2.
\]
It is recorded as the paper's identification, not adopted as an interacting-gas expectation at the bounce.

Equation **(10)**:
\[
\tilde\epsilon=-\tilde p=-\alpha n^2,\qquad \alpha=\frac9{16}\kappa.
\tag{10}
\]
The printed Friedmann pair:
\[
\dot a^2+k=\frac13\kappa(\epsilon-\alpha n^2)a^2.
\tag{11}
\]
\[
\dot a^2+2a\ddot a+k=-\kappa(p+\alpha n^2)a^2.
\tag{12}
\]
No separately numbered acceleration-only Raychaudhuri formula appears in this passage; (12) is retained rather than replaced by a new derivation.

Thermal definitions:
\[
\epsilon=\frac{\pi^2}{30}g_\star(T)T^4,\quad p=\epsilon/3,\quad
n=\frac{\zeta(3)}{\pi^2}g_n(T)T^3,\quad
g_\star=g_b+\frac78g_f,\quad g_n=\frac34g_f,
\]
\[
h_\star=\frac{\pi^2}{30}g_\star,\qquad h_n=\frac{\zeta(3)}{\pi^2}g_n.
\]
g_b and g_f sum spin-state counts over relativistic bosonic/fermionic species; h★ and h_n are treated as constant in the chosen range.

\[
\frac{dT}{T}-\frac{3\alpha h_n^2}{2h_\star}T\,dT+\frac{da}{a}=0.
\tag{14}
\]
\[
a=\frac{a_rT_r}{T}\exp\left(\frac{3\alpha h_n^2}{4h_\star}T^2\right).
\tag{15}
\]
\[
T_{\rm cr}=\left(\frac{2h_\star}{3\alpha h_n^2}\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{16}
\]
\[
a_{\rm cr}=a_rT_r\left(\frac{3e\alpha h_n^2}{2h_\star}\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{17}
\]
a_r is described as the scale factor at reference temperature T_r. The unnumbered condition directly before (16) is **da/dT(T_cr)=0**. The text identifies a cusp, not a stationary ȧ=0 minimum.

The separately printed stationary-state temperature is
\[
T_{\rm st}=\left(\frac{h_\star}{\alpha h_n^2}\right)^{1/2}>T_{\rm cr}.
\tag{23}
\]
It follows in the text from ȧ=0 in (11) with k omitted. It is not (16). S3's full text supplies neither shear evolution nor a βH⁴ production law.

### S4 — APS review, pp. 409–410

S4 uses **k** for gravitational coupling and **κ** for convergence (negative volume expansion), unlike Popławski. Timelike convention: u_i u^i=−1. The source's Riemannian derivative marker is rendered as \(\nabla^{\{\}}\).

\[
\dot\kappa:=u^i\nabla^{\{\}}_i\kappa
=-\nabla^{\{\}}_i\dot u^i+\frac{\kappa^2}{3}
+2(\sigma^2-\omega^2)+k\widetilde E.
\tag{5.21}
\]
\(\dot u^i=u^k\nabla^{\{\}}_k u^i\) is acceleration; σ, ω are shear/vorticity.
The unnumbered matter-source definition is
\[
\widetilde E=(\sigma_{ij}-g_{ij}\sigma^k{}_k/2)u^iu^j,
\]
where σ_ij is the combined energy–momentum tensor, distinct from shear σ.

\[
\widetilde E_{\rm dust}=(\rho+3P)/2-2ks^2
-2\tau^{kl}\partial_{[k}u_{l]}-\nabla^{\{\}}_k\tau^k.
\tag{5.22}
\]
ρ=−p_i u^i is mass density, P hydrostatic pressure, s² squared spin density in S4's normalization, τ^{kl} spin density, τ^k polarization current. The spin correction is −2ks². The following text gives the unnumbered condition 4ks²>ρ for negative \(\widetilde E\), with pressure, vorticity and polarization currents absent.

Visually transcribed from printed p. 410:
\[
3\left(\frac{\dot a}{a}\right)^2+
\frac{k\rho_{m0}a_0^3}{a^3}+
\frac{k\rho_{r0}a_0^4}{a^4}+
\frac{2\sigma_0^2a_0^6}{a^6}-
\frac{k^2s_0^2a_0^6}{a^6}=0.
\tag{5.24}
\]
**The leading plus sign is as printed, not repaired.** The spatially flat anisotropic metric is (5.20); \(a=(a_{11})^{1/3}(a_{33})^{1/6}\). Subscript 0 denotes present values: spinning-dust density ρ_m0, electromagnetic/radiation density ρ_r0, shear σ₀, spin density s₀, radius a₀. Numerators are constants in this example. This published shear-plus-spin equation is not a solved system with a production law.

## (C) Printed limitations and approximation statements

**Quotation boundary:** complete reproduction of every copyrighted prose passage is not provided. Short quotations are followed by explicitly labeled paraphrases with exact source locations. This section does **not** claim to meet the “every sentence, quoted” requirement. Equations in B and the authorized local text in D are transcribed directly.

### S1

Short quotation, §11 [p30](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#p30), also present in the publisher version:

> This scenario is only approximate.

**Paraphrase inventory of every explicit geometry/treatment approximation passage** (paragraph IDs are from arXiv v2; corresponding publisher sections checked):

- Abstract final sentence: KS is not attained; replace it with geometry approaching a 3-sphere.
- §1 [p8](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#p8), final sentence: torsion/production prevent exact KS; generalize for a closed FLRW limit.
- §8 [p22](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#p22), introduction to (33): production law is phenomenological.
- §10 [p27](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#p27), first three sentences: approximately constant T, H and energy density during the proposed inflation.
- §10 [p28](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#p28), sentences 2–4, citing (11): Y Ÿ≫1 supports approximate proportionality of X, Y and a; rescaling gives X≈Y.
- §11 [p30](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#p30), all four sentences: approximate illustrative scenario; Schwarzschild/KS topology R×S² is not reached with a bounce; Bianchi IX is proposed for an S³ limit.

Related restrictions: §1 p4 neglects low-density torsion; §9 uses relativistic thermal expressions; §10 p26 discusses both insufficient production and excessive production leading to eternal inflation.

### S2

Short quotation, §VIII [S8.p4](https://arxiv.org/html/2307.12190v1#S8.p4):

> A more realistic scenario of gravitational collapse should involve a fluid sphere that is inhomogeneous and rotating.

**Paraphrase inventory:** S2 does not repeat S1's explicit approximate-KS sentence.

- §II [S2.p5](https://arxiv.org/html/2307.12190v1#S2.p5), before (7)–(8): point-particle approximation in multipole conservation laws.
- §III opening: initially resting homogeneous sphere; no pressure gradients permit synchronous comoving coordinates.
- §V [S5.p2](https://arxiv.org/html/2307.12190v1#S5.p2), after (31): ultrarelativistic kinetic equilibrium and R-independent T.
- §V (34)–(36): separable closed-isotropic solution is chosen.
- §V before (40): torsion term neglected in the initial evaluation of (38).
- §VII [S7.p1](https://arxiv.org/html/2307.12190v1#S7.p1), (42): phenomenological production.
- §VIII [S8.p2](https://arxiv.org/html/2307.12190v1#S8.p2), first three sentences: T approximately constant, H and energy density nearly constant.
- §VIII S8.p4, sentences 1–4: inhomogeneity changes coordinates and radial dependence; rotation adds complications and angular momentum. Sentence 5 asserts persistence of the mechanism without solving that generalization.

Related restrictions: §I neglects low-density torsion; §VIII S8.p1 requires the maximum dimensionless production ratio in (43) below one.

### S3

Short quotation, p. 107502-2:

> The particle approximation for Dirac fields, however, is not self-consistent.

**Paraphrases:** p. 107502-2 discusses spin-fluid modeling and its cosmological-principle problem, adopts closed homogeneous isotropic geometry, neglects the early cosmological constant, assumes ultrarelativistic kinetic equilibrium and constant h★/h_n. Page 107502-3 drops k in constructing (18) and (23), restricts the temperature branch in (16)–(17), and identifies a cusp. None supplies an interacting thermal closure.

### S4

Short quotation, §V.B.7, p. 409:

> Of course, such an assumption must be justified physically.

The antecedent is inverse-volume-squared scaling for randomly oriented spins. **Paraphrases of route-relevant limitations:**

- §V.B.6, p. 408: classical spinning dust models Dirac matter; no satisfactory Lagrangian fixes a unique minimal-coupling prescription.
- §V.B.7, pp. 408–409: averaging and quadratic scaling require assumptions; high-density modeling neglects nongravitational interactions.
- §V.C opening, p. 409: restrictive shear/radiation and matter assumptions; not a comprehensive solution review.
- §V.C.1: metric symmetries and spin distributions must be compatible.
- §V.C.2, pp. 409–410: theorem hypotheses matter; footnote 17 restricts discussion to metric singularities; small polarization current is an expectation; high-density spinning dust may be inappropriate.
- §V.C.3, p. 410: nonquantized gravity has limited applicability at the example's small radii; vorticity terms are estimates.
- §V.D.1, p. 411: later bounce/horizon behavior and matching a spin-fluid interior to Kerr remain unresolved.

Homogeneous Dirac fields, including Bianchi I, are discussed separately from Weyssenhoff dust in §V.C.2, (5.23). That is not a finite-temperature interacting spin closure at the bounce.

S5/S6/S2J: full-text limitations not checked. S5's abstract describes a spin-fluid approximation; this cannot certify an exhaustive inventory.

